{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sedate-villainous-teledu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nCompletion verification currently pays to materialize the full Git-backed ticket store even though a run normally reads a small subset. Observed close traces attributed 135-235 seconds to that materialization. The approved research on `shaven-censorable-sifaka` measured the real store instead: capture of the tracker OID took p50 9.5 ms, individual tree/object reads took about 11 ms p50, and fetching plus uncached reduction of one real ticket took 29.156 ms for 10 files / 62,426 bytes with exact state-hash parity. A pinned revision stayed byte-identical after a normal live rebar write advanced the tracker.\n\nThe close path also publishes a PASS `COMPLETION_VERDICT`, the closing `STATUS`, and the completion `SIGNATURE` through separate writes. A durable PASS can therefore outlive a failed close, and a closed ticket can outlive a failed signature. The implementation needs one stable read basis and one atomic publication boundary.\n\n## User Outcome\nA completion verifier reads a stable ticket universe without copying the whole store or blocking normal writers. When the gated close succeeds, verdict sidecar, status, and signature are durably present together; when any part fails or conflicts, none of the three is committed. Multiple reviews can run concurrently without sharing mutable per-run state.\n\n## Definitions and Boundaries\n- `code_oid` is the immutable code revision resolved once from the operation's `--ref`. `tickets_oid` is the independently captured tracker commit. They are typed separately and must never be substituted for one another.\n- The pinned ticket view is read-only and demand-driven. It reads Git trees/blobs at `tickets_oid`; it never silently falls back to the live tracker and never writes reducer/graph caches inside the immutable view.\n- A read receipt records the inputs and predicates actually consulted. It covers exact ticket state, negative/not-found reads, direct-child membership, transitive descendant reachability, outbound and inbound links by relation, relation reachability where consulted, and global resolver inputs such as `.bridge_state/bindings.json`.\n- Atomicity is defined at the committed-store boundary: one local Git commit contains the PASS sidecar, closing STATUS, and completion SIGNATURE, or HEAD and the tracker worktree/index are restored with none committed. Readers pinned to a tracker OID observe the complete old or complete new commit, never a partial bundle.\n\n## Plan\n1. Add a small pinned-ticket read abstraction backed by Git object plumbing (`ls-tree` plus batched `cat-file`) and the existing uncached reducer input seam. Resolve arbitrary requested ticket IDs/aliases/Jira bindings at `tickets_oid`; record a negative receipt entry for a missing ticket. Build any derived resolver/graph index outside the immutable tree, keyed by `tickets_oid` plus reducer/schema version. Unsupported broad queries fail closed with a typed error instead of reading live state.\n2. Thread the view through completion orchestration, deterministic ticket checks, context assembly, and agent ticket tools. Keep code reads on the separate `code_oid` snapshot handle. `shimmery-customary-dorking` independently verifies the suspected live-code gap; this story must preserve the typed separation but does not assume that bug is reproduced.\n3. Validate the receipt against a current descendant Tn before mutation. Reject non-ancestor history. Diff changed ticket directories and global resolver files, then recompute affected predicates at the old/new endpoints. An unrelated mutation may continue; a relevant exact, hierarchy, link, negative, or global-resolution change discards the verdict and restarts from Tn.\n4. Prepare the verdict, receipt digest, completion certificate payload, event UUIDs, and all non-HLC work outside the global write lock. Each run owns `(run_id, code_oid, tickets_oid, receipt_digest)` and unique temporary paths. Preserve HLC monotonicity, parent STATUS linkage, authorship signing, and existing reducer semantics when final fields are assigned.\n5. Extend the status transaction seam to accept the three prepared completion-close events as one bundle. Inside the lock, assert live tracker HEAD still equals validated Tn and root status is still `in_progress`; assign final ordering/signatures, stage all three event files, and make one local Git commit. A fault before or during staging, signing, index update, or commit rolls back only this bundle and restores the pre-operation HEAD/index/worktree. Release the lock before push.\n6. Push the complete commit with ordinary fast-forward semantics. A remote rejection fetches current state, revalidates the receipt, and either rebuilds/retries an unrelated delta or restarts verification on a relevant delta. Never force a ref update or push. Same-ticket races are idempotent only when the existing close binds the equivalent code OID, tickets OID/receipt, and attestation; losing runs leave no PASS sidecar.\n7. Add phase metrics for ticket-OID capture, lazy object reads/reduction, receipt validation, bundle preparation, lock wait/hold, local commit, and push/retry. Keep metric recording outside the critical section except constant-time timestamps. Document the receipt vocabulary, atomicity boundary, retry/back-out behavior, and the intentional distinction between code and ticket revisions.\n\n## Rollout / Back-out\nShip the new ticket-view and bundled-close path behind one completion-gate configuration switch with the legacy path retained as an explicit operator back-out during dogfooding. Selection is fixed at operation start and recorded in telemetry; a run never silently falls back after it has captured `tickets_oid`. Enabling by default is a separate evidence-based rollout after concurrency/fault-injection results. The mechanism is in-process and CI-provider-independent.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Given tracker revision A and a later normal write B, a completion run pinned to A returns byte/state-identical ticket reads while live operations continue on B, without materializing the full ticket tree.\n- [ ] Any ticket requested at runtime is loaded from A and appended to the receipt; a missing ticket returns not-found-at-A and records a negative dependency, with no live fallback.\n- [ ] Direct and transitive hierarchy plus inbound/outbound linked-ticket predicates invalidate on relevant new, removed, reparented, or relinked tickets, while a proven unrelated ticket mutation remains valid.\n- [ ] Alias/Jira/global binding resolution is pinned to A; a relevant `.bridge_state/bindings.json` change invalidates the receipt. Unsupported query types fail closed.\n- [ ] Completion code evidence remains bound to the separately resolved `code_oid`; tests make swapping code and ticket roots unrepresentable or immediately rejected.\n- [ ] The write lock excludes LLM work, lazy reads/reduction, ordinary receipt validation, and push; it contains only the final HEAD/status assertions, final event ordering/signing, bundle staging, and one local commit. Lock wait and hold duration are measured.\n- [ ] On a successful gated close, exactly one local Git commit contains PASS `COMPLETION_VERDICT`, `STATUS in_progress -> closed`, and completion `SIGNATURE`, all bound to the same run/code/receipt identity.\n- [ ] Deterministic fault injection at each signing, staging, index, and commit boundary proves that failure leaves the prior HEAD/status and no committed sidecar or signature; retry creates at most one equivalent bundle.\n- [ ] Two parallel reviews of the same ticket cannot publish mixed artifacts: one wins, and the loser either recognizes an equivalent idempotent close or revalidates/restarts without leaving a PASS sidecar. Reviews of unrelated tickets may both commit after ordinary CAS revalidation.\n- [ ] A non-ancestor tracker revision, relevant receipt change, or rejected remote fast-forward fails closed without a force command; an unrelated descendant delta is safely revalidated and retried.\n- [ ] Derived indexes/caches are keyed by immutable tracker OID plus schema version and use per-run temporary names; no writes occur inside the pinned ticket view.\n- [ ] Focused concurrency, hierarchy/link phantom, negative-read, global-binding, rollback/fault-injection, and cross-process tests pass in CI, followed by the project lint, typecheck, and test gates.\n- [ ] Documentation records the stable-read contract, complete receipt vocabulary, committed-store atomicity guarantee, rollout/back-out switch, remote-writer limitation, and metric phase names.\n\n## Out of Scope\n- Replacing Git as the authoritative ticket store.\n- Treating a mutable working tree as the concurrency authority or reducing the lock to `git update-ref` alone; that requires a separate object-database-authoritative design.\n- Fixing the hypothesized pinned-code routing bug before its separate experiment proves it.\n- Fixing the per-criterion `.tmp` collision before `lineny-barathea-ladybug` proves it.\n- Establishing a numeric lock-duration SLO before the new phase instrumentation produces a distribution.","id":"b03c-f8d4-ef82-4618","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["architecture","performance","completion-verifier","concurrency"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Use a lazy pinned ticket view and atomically publish completion-close artifacts"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787679134292977001,"uuid":"5350e861-615a-4c32-b5fc-f73e2dadfa70"}